{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"submission.json\", \"summary\": \"Submitted draft/submission.json (action=check) with draft/check-plan-two.json and draft/check-harness-two.go: a combined product that states the obligation the third confirmed defect left as its remaining discriminator and runs its first direct check. The obligation c-at-most-one-leader-per-term (consensus_safety) fixes Raft's central safety property for this implementation — a term must have at most one leader, where leadership is established by counting a quorum of grants and recording the node itself as the known leader — and binds the vote rule, the transfer exemption and the candidate path; it is registered on principal fact f-known-leader (establishment) with b-leader-identity and b-vote-grant under A1/A2, b-elect-self and b-heartbeat-fastpath as support, and bound by bind-raft-elect-self-transfer at the electSelf declaration. The check chains the three previously confirmed mechanisms with public APIs only: a heartbeat-shaped AppendEntries carrying a stale term is held inside a lagging voter's StableStore CurrentTerm write (so that voter grants the next election, which proposes one above it), a third voter is unreachable for exactly that election so its vote record for the term stays empty, the release drops the lagging voter's published term by one, and the leader's transfer to it (LeadershipTransferToServer, with the leader's own intervals lengthened through ReloadConfig so no further AppendEntries reaches the target inside the window) makes it propose the term it already voted in, overwrite its record with a self-vote, and win a second quorum from the abstaining voter. The oracle is the property's negation with an exact applicability: the result event reports the term in which the transfer target advertised Leader together with the other node's own published state and term read at that instant through public accessors inside an observer filter, applicability restricts judgement to the case where the other node still held Leader in the admitted term, and the assertion requires the two terms to differ, so equality means one term had two leaders. I verified the exact submitted harness bytes in a fresh copy of the captured module: the test passes (exit 0) and emits first_leader_adopted (d1, Leader, term 3) followed by second_leader_observed (t1, Leader, term 3, other_node_state Leader, other_node_term 3, witness_recorded true), reproducible in five consecutive construction runs; the violation therefore lives in the measured comparison, not in an exit code. Counterevidence and limits are recorded explicitly: the dual leadership is transient because the two leaders revert to follower when they exchange traffic, so the observer captures the instant the second leadership is created; the abstaining voter is produced by a blocked endpoint and the lagging term by seeded stores and a delayed write, each on an injected dependency; every other vote decision remains conservative; and a lasting split, conflicting committed entries and any client-visible consequence are left as explicit unknowns and limits. The check's plan-level conflict (the transfer exemption leaves each voter's own record as the only barrier to a second quorum) is recorded so it will arrive as an applicability issue for the correspondence review, which is the next step. Optional preflight with the trusted validator returned valid=true.\"}"}}
